## Filter Users by Email Domain

Here's a clean PHP function that filters users by the `@example.com` email domain:

```php
<?php

/**
 * Filters a list of users, returning only those with an @example.com email address.
 *
 * @param array $users List of associative arrays, each containing 'name' and 'email' keys.
 * @return array Filtered list of users with @example.com email addresses.
 */
function filterExampleComUsers(array $users): array
{
    return array_values(
        array_filter($users, function (array $user): bool {
            return isset($user['email'])
                && str_ends_with(strtolower(trim($user['email'])), '@example.com');
        })
    );
}
```

### Breakdown

| Part | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `array_filter()` | Keeps only elements where the callback returns `true` |
| `isset($user['email'])` | Guards against missing `email` keys |
| `strtolower()` | Makes the check case-insensitive (e.g. `@Example.COM`) |
| `trim()` | Removes accidental leading/trailing whitespace |
| `str_ends_with()` | Cleanly checks the email suffix (PHP 8.0+) |
| `array_values()` | Re-indexes the array after filtering |

---

### Usage Example

```php
$users = [
    ['name' => 'Alice',   'email' => 'alice@example.com'],
    ['name' => 'Bob',     'email' => 'bob@gmail.com'],
    ['name' => 'Charlie', 'email' => 'charlie@example.com'],
    ['name' => 'Diana',   'email' => 'diana@yahoo.com'],
    ['name' => 'Eve',     'email' => 'eve@Example.COM'],   // case-insensitive match
    ['name' => 'Frank',   'email' => '  frank@example.com '], // whitespace trimmed
    ['name' => 'Ghost'],                                    // missing email key
];

$filtered = filterExampleComUsers($users);

print_r($filtered);
```

**Output:**
```
Array
(
    [0] => Array ( [name] => Alice   [email] => alice@example.com )
    [1] => Array ( [name] => Charlie [email] => charlie@example.com )
    [2] => Array ( [name] => Eve     [email] => eve@Example.COM )
    [3] => Array ( [name] => Frank   [email] =>   frank@example.com  )
)
```

---

### PHP 7.x Compatible Version

If you're on PHP 7.x (where `str_ends_with` isn't available), replace it with `substr()`:

```php
function filterExampleComUsers(array $users): array
{
    return array_values(
        array_filter($users, function (array $user): bool {
            if (!isset($user['email'])) {
                return false;
            }
            $email = strtolower(trim($user['email']));
            return substr($email, -strlen('@example.com')) === '@example.com';
        })
    );
}
```

